course: dictionary
lesson: 2336
title: Crumbly and what it tells us
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Crumbly describes something that breaks easily into small pieces called crumbs. «The scone was warm and crumbly.» You can picture it falling apart on your plate, so crumbly means easy to break into bits.
explain: Crumbly can also describe soil, rock or old walls. «Dark crumbly topsoil» is soil that breaks up easily in your hands. A «crumbly old wall» is one that is falling apart. Check the words around it: old, dark, layers and burrows are all clues.
explain: Use the swap check. Put "easy to break into crumbs" into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have the meaning. Context can also add hints: a «crumbly old castle wall» suggests the wall is very old and maybe unsafe.
fact: Crumbly is an adjective, and its forms are crumbly, crumblier and crumbliest.
fact: «Crumbly» means easy to break into small pieces called crumbs.
fact: «Crumbly soil» and «crumbly rock» break up easily, and scientists say they weather fast.
fact: «Crumbly biscuit» and «crumbly cheese» are phrases used about food, especially in cooking.
fact: A «crumbly old wall» is falling apart, which tells the reader it is very old and maybe unsafe.
exercise: «Worms pull dead leaves down into their burrows and leave rich crumbly droppings.» What does crumbly mean in this sentence? | *Easy to break into small pieces | Hard as stone | Shiny and smooth | Wet and sticky
exercise: What does a «crumbly old castle wall» most likely look like? | Covered in snow | *Very old and falling apart | Painted bright blue | Very new and very strong
exercise: A cook says a scone is «nice and crumbly». What does she mean? | Shiny and smooth | Wet and sticky | Hard and cold | *Light and easy to break into bits
test: «If a bar left in a hot car turns grey and crumbly, it is still safe to eat.» What does crumbly mean here? | *Breaking easily into pieces | Shiny and hard | Warm and soft | Very sweet
test: Which phrase suggests a wall is very old? | Bright red brick | *Crumbly old wall | Brand new paint | Smooth glass window
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of crumbly in a sentence? | Skip it and never come back | Count the letters in the word | Look only at the first letter | *Swap in "easy to break into crumbs" and see if the sentence still makes sense
